<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.CmdInject.Loopback.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Versions come from the repo-root Directory.Packages.props (F-38, central package management). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <PackageReference Include="NModbus" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    L5 — THE ONLY TEST PROJECT IN THIS TOOL THAT OPENS A SOCKET, AND IT IS A SEPARATE PROJECT FOR THAT
    REASON ALONE.

    Harness.CmdInject.Tests must stay socket-free: its whole value is that "nothing opened" is asserted
    against a counter on a port that CANNOT open anything. Putting a real listener in that assembly would
    make every one of those assertions weaker by association, and would make a flaky network stack able to
    redden the suite that proves the fence.

    What this project buys that the pure suite cannot: the frames the dry run PRINTS are compared against
    the registers a REAL Modbus server RECEIVED, through the real NModbus encode/decode, over a real
    (loopback-only) TCP socket. Before this, the byte dump was a claim about our own formatting code.

    The listener binds IPAddress.Loopback and an EPHEMERAL port: nothing is reachable off this machine and
    no fixed port can collide with a real service. NModbus is referenced here, and only here among the
    test projects, to HOST the slave — the client half still comes from Harness.Wire, unchanged.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.CmdInject\Harness.CmdInject.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
